            "x_max": 0.2434,
            "y_max": 0.2682,
            "width": 0.1699,
            "height": 0.0146
          },
          "confidence": 0.9887
        },
        {
          "text": "The body cavity of frogs accommodate different organ systems such as\ndigestive, circulatory, respiratory, nervous, excretory and reproductive\nsystems with well developed structures and functions (Figure 7.2).",
          "bbox": {
            "x_min": 0.0718,
            "y_min": 0.2802,
            "x_max": 0.697,
            "y_max": 0.3278,
            "width": 0.6252,
            "height": 0.0476
          },
          "confidence": 0.996
        },
        {
          "text": "The digestive system consists of alimentary canal and digestive glands.\nThe alimentary canal is short because frogs are carnivores and hence the\nlength of intestine is reduced. The mouth opens into the buccal cavity that\nleads to the oesophagus through pharynx. Oesophagus is a short tube\nthat opens into the stomach which in turn continues as the intestine, rectum\nand finally opens outside by the cloaca. Liver secretes bile that is stored in\nthe gall bladder. Pancreas, a digestive gland produces pancreatic juice",
          "bbox": {
            "x_min": 0.0712,
            "y_min": 0.3303,
            "x_max": 0.6976,
            "y_max": 0.4404,
            "width": 0.6264,
