        },
        {
          "text": "BIOLOGY",
          "bbox": {
            "x_min": 0.857,
            "y_min": 0.084,
            "x_max": 0.9253,
            "y_max": 0.093,
            "width": 0.0683,
            "height": 0.009
          },
          "confidence": 0.9813
        },
        {
          "text": "containing digestive enzymes. Food is captured by the bilobed tongue.\nDigestion of food takes place by the action of HCl and gastric juices secreted\nfrom the walls of the stomach. Partially digested food called chyme is passed\nfrom stomach to the first part of the small intestine, the duodenum. The\nduodenum receives bile from gall bladder and pancreatic juices from the\npancreas through a common bile duct. Bile emulsifies fat and pancreatic\njuices digest carbohydrates and proteins. Final digestion takes place in the\nintestine. Digested food is absorbed by the numerous finger-like folds in\nthe inner wall of intestine called villi and microvilli. The undigested solid\nwaste moves into the rectum and passes out through cloaca.",
          "bbox": {
            "x_min": 0.3001,
            "y_min": 0.135,
            "x_max": 0.9259,
            "y_max": 0.2922,
            "width": 0.6258,
            "height": 0.1572
          },
          "confidence": 0.9964
        },
        {
          "text": "Frogs respire on land and in the water by two different methods. In\nwater, skin acts as aquatic respiratory organ (cutaneous respiration).\nDissolved oxygen in the water is exchanged through the skin by diffusion.\nOn land, the buccal cavity, skin and lungs act as the respiratory organs.\nThe respiration by lungs is called pulmonary respiration. The lungs are a\npair of elongated, pink coloured sac-like structures present in the upper\npart of the trunk region (thorax). Air enters through the nostrils into the\nbuccal cavity and then to lungs. During aestivation and hibernation\ngaseous exchange takes place through skin.",
          "bbox": {
            "x_min": 0.3012,
            "y_min": 0.2952,
            "x_max": 0.9276,
            "y_max": 0.4366,
            "width": 0.6264,
            "height": 0.1414
          },
          "confidence": 0.9961
        },
        {
          "text": "The vascular system of frog is well-developed closed type. Frogs have\na lymphatic system also. The blood vascular system involves heart, blood\nvessels and blood. The lymphatic system consists of lymph, lymph\nchannels and lymph nodes. Heart is a muscular structure situated in the\nupper part of the body cavity. It has three chambers, two atria and one\nventricle and is covered by a membrane called pericardium. A triangular\nstructure called sinus venosus joins the right atrium. It receives blood\nthrough the major veins called vena cava. The ventricle opens into a sac-\nlike conus arteriosus on the ventral side of the heart. The blood from the\nheart is carried to all parts of the body by the arteries (arterial system).\nThe veins collect blood from different parts of body to the heart and form\nthe venous system. Special venous connection between liver and intestine\nas well as the kidney and lower parts of the body are present in frogs. The\nformer is called hepatic portal system and the latter is called renal portal\nsystem. The blood is composed of plasma and cells. The blood cells are\nRBC (red blood cells) or erythrocytes, WBC (white blood cells) or leucocytes\nand platelets. RBC's are nucleated and contain red coloured pigment\nnamely haemoglobin. The lymph is different from blood. It lacks few\nproteins and RBCs. The blood carries nutrients, gases and water to the\nrespective sites during the circulation. The circulation of blood is achieved\nby the pumping action of the muscular heart.",
          "bbox": {
            "x_min": 0.3012,
            "y_min": 0.4392,
            "x_max": 0.927,
            "y_max": 0.7742,
            "width": 0.6258,
            "height": 0.335
          },
          "confidence": 0.9955
        },
        {
          "text": "The elimination of nitrogenous wastes is carried out by a well\ndeveloped excretory system. The excretory system consists of a pair of\nkidneys, ureters, cloaca and urinary bladder. These are compact, dark\nred and bean like structures situated a little posteriorly in the body cavity\non both sides of vertebral column. Each kidney is composed of several\nstructural and functional units called uriniferous tubules or nephrons.\nTwo ureters emerge from the kidneys in the male frogs. The ureters act as\nurinogenital duct which opens into the cloaca. In females the ureters and",
          "bbox": {
            "x_min": 0.3012,
            "y_min": 0.7763,
            "x_max": 0.9282,
            "y_max": 0.9015,
            "width": 0.627,
            "height": 0.1251
          },
          "confidence": 0.997
        },
        {
          "text": "not",
          "bbox": {
