            "y_min": 0.2326,
            "x_max": 0.7104,
            "y_max": 0.2549,
            "width": 0.0806,
            "height": 0.0223
          },
          "confidence": 0.9928
        },
        {
          "text": "The system for control and coordination is highly evolved in the frog. It\nincludes both neural system and endocrine glands. The chemical\ncoordination of various organs of the body is\nachieved by hormones which are secreted by the\nendocrine glands. The prominent endocrine\nglands found in frog are pituitary, thyroid,\nparathyroid, thymus, pineal body, pancreatic\nislets, adrenals and gonads. The nervous system\nis organised into a central nervous system (brain\nand spinal cord), a peripheral nervous system\n(cranial and spinal nerves) and an autonomic\nnervous system (sympathetic and\nparasympathetic). There are ten pairs of cranial gland",
          "bbox": {
            "x_min": 0.0718,
            "y_min": 0.1988,
            "x_max": 0.697,
            "y_max": 0.4049,
            "width": 0.6252,
            "height": 0.2061
          },
          "confidence": 0.9927
        },
        {
          "text": "nerves arising from the brain. Brain is enclosed\nin a bony structure called brain box (cranium).\nThe brain is divided into fore-brain, mid-brain\nand hind-brain. Forebrain includes olfactory\nlobes, paired cerebral hemispheres and unpaired\ndiencephalon. The midbrain is characterised by\na pair of optic lobes. Hind-brain consists of\ncerebellum and medulla oblongata. The medulla\noblongata passes out through the foramen\nmagnum and continues into spinal cord, which\nis enclosed in the vertebral column.",
          "bbox": {
            "x_min": 0.0712,
            "y_min": 0.407,
            "x_max": 0.4828,
            "y_max": 0.5784,
            "width": 0.4116,
            "height": 0.1714
          },
          "confidence": 0.9941
        },
        {
          "text": "Frog has different types of sense organs, namely\norgans of touch (sensory papillae), taste (taste\nbuds), smell (nasal epithelium), vision (eyes) and\nhearing (tympanum with internal ears). Out of\nthese, eyes and internal ears are well-organised\nstructures and the rest are cellular aggregations\naround nerve endings. Eyes in a frog are a pair of\nspherical structures situated in the orbit in skull.\nThese are simple eyes (possessing only one unit).\nExternal ear is absent in frogs and only tympanum\ncan be seen externally. The ear is an organ of\nhearing as well as balancing (equilibrium).",
          "bbox": {
            "x_min": 0.0712,
            "y_min": 0.5835,
            "x_max": 0.4834,
            "y_max": 0.7738,
            "width": 0.4121,
            "height": 0.1902
          },
          "confidence": 0.995
        },
        {
          "text": "Frogs have well organised male and female\nreproductive systems. Male reproductive organs\nconsist of a pair of yellowish ovoid testes (Figure\n7.3), which are found adhered to the upper part\nof kidneys by a double fold of peritoneum called\nmesorchium. Vasa efferentia are 10-12 in\nnumber that arise from testes. They enter the\nkidneys on their side and open into Bidder's",
